Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

map simple algorithm steps to Python statements;
predict the order in which a sequential program executes;
use print() to make program output visible;
distinguish an algorithmic idea from its Python representation;
identify simple execution failures caused by imprecise code;
modify and build a short sequential Python program.

Concepts to keep

algorithm;
program;
Python statement;
execution order;
sequential execution;
output;
print();
prediction;
tracing;
translation from algorithm to code.

Class technology
You will normally use:

a web browser;
Google Colab;
the DSA105 course site;
Google Forms;
Google Meet as a classroom screen mirror when needed.

When the instructor shares the classroom computer through Google Meet, join with your microphone and speakers muted unless told otherwise.

Before using an AI assistant
Produce at least one of the following yourself:

a problem representation;
pseudocode;
a prediction;
a partial implementation;
a test case;
an error diagnosis.

Acceptable learning roles
AI may be used for:

explanation;
debugging support;
syntax/code completion;
alternative solutions;
comparison of implementations.

When AI use is part of a homework task, you may be asked to state:

what you had produced before asking AI;
what you accepted;
what you rejected or changed;
how you verified the result.

Evidence sources
Your portfolio may include:

quiz responses;
selected lab evidence;
exit tickets;
homework tasks;
self-assessment;
recurring misconception patterns.

What feedback can look like
A portfolio may identify:

secure skills;
developing skills;
recurring misconceptions;
recommended next practice;
differences between self-perception and observed evidence.

Individual student data are not published on this website.

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish a problem, an algorithm, and a program;
describe a simple problem using Input → Process → Output;
write a precise and ordered sequence of steps;
interpret a basic flowchart;
connect an algorithm to a minimal executable Python representation.

Before class
Think about one everyday process that seems simple to a human but would require very precise instructions for a computer.
Examples:

calculating a receipt;
determining a discount;
finding an average;
processing an order.

Bring one example to class.

Concepts to keep

problem;
input;
process;
output;
algorithm;
sequence;
precision;
flowchart;
program;
executable representation.

Learning goals
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

distinguish a value from the name used to refer to it;
create and update simple Python variables;
identify common data types: int, float, str, and bool;
use type() to inspect how Python represents a value;
explain why "120" and 120 are not the same representation;
trace simple assignment and reassignment step by step.

Concepts to keep

value;
variable;
name;
assignment;
reassignment;
int;
float;
str;
bool;
type();
representation;
tracing.
